       here: vertical rms 0.1362 (canonical) / 0.1319 (alt); h117 reports 0.1327 / 0.1326
  [PASS] C5 [control] an INDEPENDENT script in this repository (h117, different loader, different author-pass) gets the same parameter-free vertical scatter on the same cut, to better than 0.005 dex   (canonical 0.1362 vs 0.1327, alt 0.1319 vs 0.1326; point counts 2684 vs 2684)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
C4 [control] -- is a_0 a hidden fit?  free the ONE global parameter and see what it buys
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  one global free a_0: log10 a = -9.9455 (a = 1.134e-10 m/s^2), rms 0.1421 dex
     distance from the frozen footings: alt +0.002 dex, canonical +0.083 dex
  [PASS] C4 [control] the FROZEN a_0 is not a hidden per-sample fit: freeing the one global scale buys less than 0.005 dex over the frozen alt footing   (frozen alt 0.1421 dex, free 0.1421 dex, gain 0.0000 dex.  NOTE AGAINST INTEREST: a_0 was originally calibrated on galaxy dynamics, so 'zero free parameters' means zero parameters RE-FITTED here, not a parameter never touched by data of this kind)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
F1 [LIKE-FOR-LIKE] -- let each galaxy's halo float within its REAL priors, as a LambdaCDM analysis would
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Abundance matching predicts the halo POPULATION, never an individual curve.  The real LambdaCDM claim is
  that a halo exists inside the population scatter that fits.  So the halo is refit per galaxy in three
  regimes, and the kernel is given the SAME number of parameters in the last one.
  (per-galaxy fits over 155 galaxies took 2.1 s)

      model                                   par/gal  rms [dex]  vs kernel 0-par
      framework kernel, a_0 fixed (alt)             0     0.1421               --
      abundance-matched halo (L16)                  0     0.1711            1.20x
      halo, M200+c within LCDM priors               2     0.0659            0.46x
      halo, M200+c FREE                             2     0.0599            0.42x
      halo, M200+c+Ups+offset, priors               4     0.0520            0.37x
      kernel, Ups+offset, priors                    2     0.0602            0.42x
      kernel, Ups+offset FREE                       2     0.0513            0.36x
      the prior-constrained fit moves log M_200 by median -0.04 dex [-0.50, +0.24] and log c by -0.02 [-0.22, +0.16] (priors 0.25 / 0.11 dex)
      the FREE fit moves log M_200 by median +0.07 dex [-0.67, +2.21] and log c by -0.03 [-0.81, +0.30] -- how far outside the population it has to go
      IS THE FITTED HALO POPULATION STILL LambdaCDM's?  the prior-constrained fits scatter by 0.426 dex in log M_200 (prior 0.25) and 0.200 dex in log c (prior 0.11); 19% of galaxies sit beyond 2 sigma in M_200 and 28% beyond 2 sigma in c
  [FAIL] F1d [is the fit legitimate?] the halo population the per-galaxy fit lands on is no broader than the LambdaCDM population it was supposed to be drawn from -- if it is broader, the 'fitted halo' beats the kernel only by leaving the population that predicted it   (fitted scatter 0.426 / 0.200 dex against priors 0.25 / 0.11 dex)
      external reality check -- Li+2020's PUBLISHED per-galaxy fits to these same 175 curves (median reduced chi^2):
          NFW-LCDM       2.22  (n = 165)
          NFW-Flat       1.61  (n = 165)
          DC14-LCDM      1.58  (n = 165)
          Burkert-Flat   0.86  (n = 165)
          i.e. a fitted NFW halo is a GOOD fit to individual SPARC curves; that has never been in dispute.
  [FAIL] F1a [like-for-like] the fixed-a_0 kernel with ZERO free parameters is still tighter than a halo whose M_200 and c float within their REAL LambdaCDM priors (0.25 / 0.11 dex)   (kernel 0.1421 dex vs prior-constrained fitted halo 0.0659 dex)
  [FAIL] F1b [like-for-like] ... and tighter than a FREELY fitted NFW halo   (kernel 0.1421 dex vs freely fitted halo 0.0599 dex)
  [PASS] F1c [equal DOF] given the SAME two prior-constrained parameters per galaxy on each side (halo: M_200, c; kernel: Upsilon_*, distance/inclination offset), the kernel is tighter   (kernel+2 0.0602 dex vs halo+2 0.0659 dex)

  F1e -- THE DECISIVE FAIR TEST that F1d implies.  A fitted halo is only a LambdaCDM halo if the population
  it lands on has LambdaCDM's OWN width.  So tighten the priors by a common factor until the FITTED
  population's scatter equals the 0.25 / 0.11 dex LambdaCDM predicts, and read off the tightness there.
       prior shrink   sigma_M   sigma_c  fitted sd(logM)  fitted sd(logc)  rms [dex]
                1.0     0.250     0.110            0.426            0.200     0.0659
                1.5     0.167     0.073            0.354            0.166     0.0701
                2.0     0.125     0.055            0.306            0.144     0.0743
                2.5     0.100     0.044            0.272            0.128     0.0786
                3.0     0.083     0.037            0.246            0.115     0.0828
                4.0     0.062     0.028            0.209            0.096     0.0905
                6.0     0.042     0.018            0.162            0.072     0.1037
               10.0     0.025     0.011            0.107            0.046     0.1229
          1000000.0     0.000     0.000            0.000            0.000     0.1711
      interpolated: a fitted halo population carrying LambdaCDM's own sd(log M_200) = 0.25 reaches 0.0822 dex; one carrying sd(log c) = 0.11 reaches 0.0850 dex
      (CONSERVATIVE towards LambdaCDM: MAP estimates carry estimation noise ON TOP of the true population
       scatter, so matching the FITTED width to the predicted width lets the true width be smaller still)
  [FAIL] F1e [THE DECISIVE FAIR TEST] once the fitted halo population is required to have LambdaCDM's OWN width in log M_200 and log c -- not merely to start from a LambdaCDM prior and then leave it -- the zero-parameter kernel is tighter than it   (kernel 0.1421 dex vs width-matched fitted halo 0.0850 dex (M_200-matched 0.0822, c-matched 0.0850); the unconstrained-width fit reached 0.0659 dex only by scattering 1.7x / 1.8x wider than LambdaCDM predicts)

