      lowest BIC: kernel + 2/galaxy (priors) (Delta BIC to the zero-parameter kernel: +14283)
  [FAIL] F2a [BIC] with the parameter count charged for, the zero-parameter kernel is the preferred model   (kernel BIC 19871; best rival kernel + 2/galaxy (priors) at 5589)

  HELD-OUT PREDICTION -- the criterion that needs no convention.  Split each curve at its median radius,
  fit the per-galaxy parameters on one half, and score the OTHER half.  The kernel has nothing to fit, so
  its held-out score is simply its score.  Galaxies with >= 6 surviving points, >= 2 per half.
      model                                fit inner -> score outer   fit outer -> score inner
      framework kernel, 0 par                                0.1181                     0.1538
      abundance-matched halo, 0 par                          0.1730                     0.1617
      halo, 2/gal (LCDM priors)                              0.1289                     0.1172
      halo, 2/gal (width-matched)                            0.1290                     0.1270
      halo, 2/gal (free)                                     0.1591                     0.1237
      halo, 4/gal (priors)                                   0.1182                     0.1205
      kernel, 2/gal (priors)                                 0.0793                     0.1145
      (133 galaxies, 1319 / 1375 held-out points)
  [FAIL] F2b [held-out] the zero-parameter kernel predicts the held-out half of each rotation curve better than ANY fitted halo does, in BOTH directions   (in->out kernel 0.1181 vs best halo 0.1182; out->in kernel 0.1538 vs best halo 0.1172)
  [FAIL] F2c [held-out, the classic test] fitting the OUTER half and predicting the INNER half -- the direction in which a cuspy halo is known to struggle -- the zero-parameter kernel beats a freely fitted NFW   (kernel 0.1538 dex vs free NFW 0.1237 dex)
  [PASS] F2e [held-out, zero parameters both sides] the zero-parameter kernel predicts the held-out half better than the zero-parameter abundance-matched halo, in BOTH directions -- so L16's headline comparison is not an in-sample artefact   (in->out kernel 0.1181 vs halo 0.1730; out->in kernel 0.1538 vs halo 0.1617)
  [PASS] F2d [held-out, EQUAL FREEDOM -- the fair predictive comparison] given the same two prior-constrained nuisance parameters per galaxy (Upsilon_*, distance/inclination offset) fitted on one half of each curve, the kernel predicts the other half better than a halo given those SAME nuisances PLUS its own M_200 and c, in both directions   (in->out kernel+2 0.0793 vs halo+2 0.1289 and halo+4 0.1182; out->in kernel+2 0.1145 vs halo+2 0.1172 and halo+4 0.1205)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
F3 [ERROR BUDGET] -- is 0.142 dex above or below the observational floor?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Forward Monte Carlo.  For each realisation the TRUE data are generated from the model itself with true
  nuisance values drawn from SPARC's own quoted errors (per-point velocity, distance, inclination) plus a
  0.11 dex stellar Upsilon, and then analysed with the NOMINAL values, exactly as the real analysis is.
  The rms this induces is the floor: the scatter a PERFECT model would still show.
                                                     observed      floor  obs/floor  excess (quad)
      framework kernel, quoted errors                  0.1421     0.1349       1.05         0.0449
      abundance-matched halo, quoted errors            0.1711     0.1288       1.33         0.1126
      framework kernel, distance errors x0.6 (h117D)     0.1421     0.1154       1.23         0.0830
      abundance-matched halo, distance errors x0.6 (h117D)     0.1711     0.1082       1.58         0.1326
      the two floors differ by only -0.0061 dex (the halo's is marginally LOWER, because at these radii
      a dark-dominated model is less sensitive to a stellar Upsilon error than a baryon-tracking kernel is),
      so the floor is not what separates the two observed numbers.
      cross-check against h117 (independent script): vertical rms 0.133 dex on the Lelli cut, within-galaxy
      intrinsic 0.036 +- 0.008, between-galaxy intrinsic 0.078 +- 0.022 dex.
  [PASS] F3a [floor] the kernel's residual is AT the observational error floor (within 25%), i.e. there is little room left for it to be wrong   (observed 0.1421 dex, floor 0.1349 dex, ratio 1.05; excess in quadrature 0.0449 dex)
  [PASS] F3b [floor] the abundance-matched halo's residual is NOT at its floor, so the two models are distinguishable by these data rather than both being error-limited   (observed 0.1711 dex, floor 0.1288 dex, ratio 1.33; excess 0.1126 dex)

