----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
V [VERDICT] -- what can honestly be claimed
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      the claim as stated -- 'the kernel describes SPARC rotation curves more tightly than a LambdaCDM
      halo' with no condition attached -- requires the kernel to beat a FITTED halo as well as a predicted
      one.  F1a FAIL, F1b FAIL, F2a FAIL, F2b FAIL.
      NOTE AGAINST INTEREST, not tested here: NFW is the LEAST favourable LambdaCDM profile.  Li+2020's own
      feedback-modified DC14 halo with a LambdaCDM prior fits these same curves better than NFW does
      (median reduced chi^2 1.58 against 2.22), so every 'fitted halo' number above is an UPPER bound on
      what a fitted LambdaCDM halo can do.
  [FAIL] V1 [verdict] the tightness claim is publishable AS STATED, i.e. without a condition naming what the halo was and was not allowed to do   (kernel 0.1421 dex; predicted halo 0.1711; halo fitted within LambdaCDM priors 0.0659; halo fitted freely 0.0599)
  [PASS] V2 [verdict] the CONDITIONED claim -- 'at zero free parameters per galaxy on both sides, with the halo's M_200 and c PREDICTED by abundance matching rather than fitted, the fixed-a_0 kernel is tighter, and it sits at the observational error floor' -- survives every attack in this lane   (zero-parameter comparison 0.1421 vs 0.1711 (1.20x), stable across 9 cut variations, all three kernels, both footings, and at 1.05x its own error floor)
  [FAIL] V3 [verdict] the STRONGEST fair statement available -- 'given the same per-galaxy freedom, in-sample and out-of-sample, the kernel describes and PREDICTS these curves better than an NFW halo' -- survives   (in-sample equal-DOF 0.0602 vs 0.0659; held-out in->out 0.0793 vs 0.1289; held-out out->in 0.1145 vs 0.1172 -- but the in-sample margin is only 0.0057 dex and it REVERSES on the 'Q<=1 only' cut (0.0596 vs 0.0594), so the equal-DOF in-sample statement is a tie, not a win; only the held-out statement survives)
  [PASS] V4 [verdict] the PREDICTIVE equal-freedom statement -- 'fitted on half of each rotation curve with the same two nuisance parameters per galaxy, the kernel predicts the other half better than an NFW halo given those same nuisances and its own M_200 and c as well' -- survives, in both directions   (held-out in->out 0.0793 vs halo+2 0.1289 / halo+4 0.1182; out->in 0.1145 vs 0.1172 / 0.1205; and at zero parameters both sides 0.1181/0.1538 vs 0.1730/0.1617)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE FAIR STATEMENT -- what a referee would let through
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  On the 155 SPARC galaxies and 2786 points that survive the standard accuracy cut, the framework's kernel
  with a_0 frozen and Upsilon_* frozen -- nothing fitted per galaxy -- reproduces log10 g_obs to 0.142 dex,
  against 0.171 dex for an NFW halo whose M_200 and c are PREDICTED by abundance matching, and
  0.198 dex for a random draw from that halo population.  That advantage is stable across nine
  selections (ratio 1.20-1.37), holds for all three candidate readings of the frozen kernel and both
  a_0 footings, survives out of sample in both directions, and sits at 1.05x the scatter the quoted
  observational errors alone would produce -- so it is close to the best any model could do on these data.
  
  It is NOT, however, a demonstration that the kernel beats LambdaCDM, and it must not be written as one.
  Abundance matching predicts a halo POPULATION, not an individual rotation curve, and LambdaCDM has never
  claimed otherwise.  Allowed to do what it does claim -- each galaxy's halo lying somewhere in a population
  of width 0.25 dex in log M_200 and 0.11 dex in log c -- a fitted NFW halo reaches 0.085 dex, comfortably
  tighter than the kernel, and its within-galaxy SHAPE residual, 0.070 dex, is a dead heat with the
  kernel's 0.072 dex (-0.002 dex, the halo marginally ahead).
  The kernel's real and defensible content is therefore predictive, not descriptive: it delivers that
  shape-channel performance with zero parameters per galaxy where the halo needs two, it wins every
  held-out comparison at equal per-galaxy freedom, and it is the only one of the two that says in advance
  what any given curve will look like.  A referee will accept that sentence.  He will not accept '0.142
  against 0.171' without the conditions attached, because the halo he has in mind is the fitted one.

